# Insects and bats.
#
# These are the most seasonal animals in the pack. Most adult insects live
# for a few weeks or one warm season. In a cold winter there are none to
# see. So the cold groups (snowy, taiga, mountain) get zeros in winter.
#
# Rows are listed cold to mild. The first row that fits wins, so 'snowy'
# comes before 'taiga' and 'any' comes last. Some sections end with a
# 2-number 'any' row (dry wet). That row is only for the tropical biomes
# (desert, savanna, jungle, badlands, mangrove swamp). The 4-number rows
# never apply there.
#
# Two limits to know.
#   1. Biome here means the biome at the spawn spot. Lush caves and
#      dripstone caves are in the 'cave' group (biomes.rules). A cave is
#      sheltered, so the bugs that spawn in lush caves have a milder 'cave'
#      row ahead of the cold rows. Those cave numbers are guesses. Bats are the one animal
#      where it is right.
#   2. Nobody published "0.5". The source says when and how strong. The
#      size of each number is a call.
#
# The rows are copied across a lot of insects on purpose. Where a number
# is the same, the reason is the same.

[butterflies]
animals    = naturalist:butterfly
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://learnbutterflies.com/abundance-diversity-seasonality/
             https://www.birdsandblooms.com/blog/butterflies-in-winter/
why        = Cold country has almost no adult butterflies in winter. Most species
             spend the cold months as caterpillars, pupae or eggs. A few hardy kinds
             (tortoiseshells, anglewings, mourning cloaks) overwinter as adults and
             the monarch flies south. So winter is zero in snow, taiga and mountain
             country and a trace elsewhere. Adults fly from late spring and peak in
             summer. In the tropics the pattern is rain, not cold. The first page
             above (read) says butterflies in seasonal tropical forest are far more
             abundant in the wet season. They are scarce and hide in the dry season
             and come out in a mass about three to four weeks after the first rains.
             The same page says tropical seasonality is weaker than in temperate
             regions and some wet forests are even all year. So jungle only drops a
             little in the dry half. Savanna, desert, badlands and mangrove swamp
             drop a lot. The second page was seen in search results only. The
             temperate winter claim rests on it and on general knowledge.
#              spring summer autumn winter
snowy    = 0.0  0.5  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.1  0.9  0.3  0.0
mountain = 0.1  1.0  0.4  0.0
any      = 0.5  1.0  0.7  0.05
#              dry  wet
jungle   = 0.7  1.0
any      = 0.3  1.0

[fireflies]
animals    = naturalist:firefly
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://extension.okstate.edu/fact-sheets/print-publications/epp-entomology-and-plant-pathologhy/epp-7345-an-introduction-to-fireflies-a.pdf
why        = Adult fireflies live only about one to four weeks. They spend most of
             their life as larvae underground. The Oklahoma State fact sheet says the
             adults peak in May and June and are around from May to November in
             warm, humid weather. The page would not open for me. I only saw its
             summary in a search result, so this is reasoned and not sourced. Spring
             is cut because only late spring has adults. Summer is the peak.
             Autumn is a trace. A Texas report of a late-September wave says it
             happens, but it is rare. Winter is zero. Snow country is cut harder
             because the season there is shorter and later. Tropical fireflies
             (Southeast Asian mangrove kinds, for example) are around much of the
             year. I found no wet or dry timing for them. The tropical rows are a
             guess that they follow the rains, and mild.
#              spring summer autumn winter
snowy    = 0.0  0.3  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.2  0.9  0.05 0.0
mountain = 0.2  0.9  0.05 0.0
any      = 0.6  1.0  0.1  0.0
#              dry  wet
any      = 0.6  1.0

[bats]
animals    = minecraft:bat
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.ontario.ca/page/little-brown-bat
             https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/kip_articles/469/
why        = Temperate bats hibernate. The Ontario page (read) says little brown bats
             hibernate from October or November to March or April, in caves or mines
             that are humid and stay above freezing. A bat asleep in a cave is not
             a bat you meet. So winter is zero in cold country and low elsewhere.
             Spring and autumn are partial. The bats are waking in April and they
             are swarming and going in from autumn. Summer is the peak. Some
             temperate species stay active all year in warm country. A Florida
             study (seen in a search result, not read) found one that does.
             That is why the mild 'any' winter is 0.15 and not zero. The tropical
             biomes get no row at all. Tropical bats do not hibernate. They keep
             1.0 all year. I found no tropical bat page that gave a wet and dry
             split, so I did not invent one. The bat is the one animal in this
             file that lives in caves on purpose. The biome used is the cave biome
             at the spawn spot. That is right here, because a cave roost is where
             the bat season shows.
#              spring summer autumn winter
snowy    = 0.3  0.9  0.4  0.0
taiga    = 0.5  1.0  0.7  0.0
mountain = 0.5  1.0  0.7  0.0
any      = 0.8  1.0  0.9  0.15

[dragonflies]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:dragonfly
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.worlddragonfly.org/article/13887890-2011-570155
why        = In this pack the dragonfly spawns only in rivers. Temperate dragonfly
             adults fly from late spring to early autumn. Larvae live in the water
             through winter and the adults come out when it warms. The page above
             (read) is a Hong Kong study. It says flying seasons began in March or
             April, and in eight species they ran to November, and it calls the
             life cycles "intermediate" between equatorial and high latitude
             species. It is not a temperate study, so it only backs the shape. The
             May to September window comes from search results I did not open.
             Winter is zero in cold country and a trace in mild country. River
             biome itself is never tropical, so there are no dry and wet rows.
#              spring summer autumn winter
snowy    = 0.0  0.4  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.2  1.0  0.4  0.0
mountain = 0.2  1.0  0.4  0.0
any      = 0.4  1.0  0.6  0.05

[ladybugs]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:ladybug
confidence = sourced
source     = https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5999267/
why        = Adult ladybirds spend the cold months packed in groups under bark and
             leaf litter. The page above (read) followed one European species. It
             says they arrived at the winter sites from late October to early
             November and left from mid April to late April. The beetles are
             out and about from spring to autumn. In winter the sleeping groups
             are tucked away, so you rarely meet one in the open. Cold country has
             a later spring and an earlier autumn. The page is one species in one
             region, so the sizes are my call. The game spawns them in forest
             and lush caves.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 0.8  1.0  0.9  0.6
snowy    = 0.0  0.6  0.1  0.0
taiga    = 0.3  1.0  0.5  0.0
mountain = 0.3  1.0  0.5  0.0
any      = 0.6  1.0  0.8  0.1

[stag-beetles]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:stag_beetle
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.donauauen.at/en/facts/nature-science/fauna-engl/stag-beetle
             https://www.newforestnpa.gov.uk/discover/wildlife/beetles/stag-beetle
why        = Adult stag beetles live a few weeks in early summer. The larvae live
             for years in rotting wood. The Donau-Auen park page (read) says the
             flying period is from the end of May to June or July. The New Forest
             page (read) says adults are found from late May to the end of August.
             So summer is the peak, spring is a small early tail, and autumn and
             winter are near zero. In cold country the beetles come later and
             spring is zero. Zero in a mild winter is a call. Both pages are
             European (Lucanus cervus), not the game's own beetle.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 0.4  1.0  0.3  0.2
snowy    = 0.0  0.4  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.0  1.0  0.1  0.0
mountain = 0.0  1.0  0.1  0.0
any      = 0.2  1.0  0.1  0.0

[weevils]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:weevil
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://prairiepest.ca/risk-maps/cabbage-seedpod-weevil/
why        = The game has no species, only a generic weevil. Many real weevils
             overwinter as adults in leaf litter and soil. The pest pages seen in
             search results say they come out in spring when the soil warms (15 C for
             the cabbage seedpod weevil) and feed through the warm months. Some
             species overwinter as larvae instead, and the adults appear in May or
             June. So the rows are the generic version. Spring is part strength,
             summer is the peak, autumn fades, winter is mostly in shelter. None of
             the pages are about the game's animal. The weevil is reasoned
             from a few pest species.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 0.8  1.0  0.9  0.6
snowy    = 0.0  0.5  0.1  0.0
taiga    = 0.4  1.0  0.5  0.0
mountain = 0.4  1.0  0.5  0.0
any      = 0.7  1.0  0.8  0.1

[roly-polies]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:roly_poly
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://essexfieldclub.org.uk/archivetext.php/s/028/o/0141
             https://scaber.org/porcellio/woodlice/habitat/
why        = Woodlice follow moisture more than temperature. The old Essex page
             (seen in search results) says they moved to the dampest spot whatever
             the temperature. It also says in winter most species are scarce and
             found only deep in crevices, in a semi-lethargic state. A habitat page
             says a hot summer (32 C) and a cold snap (-5 C) both push them deeper
             into shelter. So hot dry summer and winter are both low. Autumn is
             damp and cool, so it is the peak. Woodlice do not need much warmth, so
             the cuts are milder than for the winged insects. I could not open
             either page in full. Reasoned.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 1.0  1.0  1.0  0.8
snowy    = 0.1  0.6  0.2  0.0
taiga    = 0.5  0.9  1.0  0.1
mountain = 0.5  0.9  1.0  0.1
any      = 0.8  0.8  1.0  0.3

[snails]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:snail
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://www.publishing.csiro.au/zo/ZO9680857
             https://meetingorganizer.copernicus.org/EGU22/EGU22-5061.html
why        = Land snails go dormant when it is too cold (hibernation) or too dry
             (aestivation). Rain brings them out. Search results said a study of a
             pest snail calls winter dormancy brief and daily, and summer
             dormancy in rare-rain country lasts days to weeks. A pest guide says
             they are mostly inactive in summer and active in moist soil the
             rest of the year. A UK garden snail study says waking from hibernation
             follows the first heavy rain in May and not the air temperature. I
             did not open any of these pages. That is why it is reasoned. Summer is
             cut for the dry heat. Winter is cut hard. Spring and autumn rain is
             the peak. The game spawns snails only in forest and lush caves. There
             is no dry and wet row because those biomes are not tropical.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 1.0  0.9  1.0  0.7
snowy    = 0.2  0.5  0.3  0.0
taiga    = 0.8  0.9  1.0  0.1
mountain = 0.8  0.9  1.0  0.1
any      = 1.0  0.7  1.0  0.3

[stick-bugs]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:stick_bug
confidence = guess
source     = none found
why        = Stick insects are mostly tropical and subtropical. A Queensland trap
             study seen in a search result found one species breeding all year
             in rainforest with no wet or dry cycle. A Panama study found the
             group poor and sparse. I found nothing on stick insects in a cold
             forest, and no page about this animal. In the game the stick bug
             spawns in forest and lush caves, not in jungle. So this is a guess
             that a forest stick bug is a summer and autumn animal that cold
             country does not hold. Winter is zero. Zero is my call, and a real
             temperate stick insect overwinters as an egg, which fits.
#              spring summer autumn winter
cave     = 0.6  1.0  0.8  0.3
snowy    = 0.0  0.2  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.1  0.6  0.4  0.0
mountain = 0.1  0.6  0.4  0.0
any      = 0.4  1.0  0.8  0.0

[leaf-insects]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:leaf_insect
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/5919/
why        = Real leaf insects live in the tropics of South and Southeast Asia.
             They do not live in cold forest. In the game they spawn in jungle and
             in forest. The page above is the Queensland trap study of a stick
             insect (not a leaf insect). I saw it only in search results. It
             found no wet or dry cycle in egg output, so jungle gets no row and
             stays at 1.0. The cold forest is the mismatch. A leaf insect would not
             survive a snowy winter, so snow country is near zero and the cold
             groups are cut hard. Leaf insects are tougher on this than stick bugs.
#              spring summer autumn winter
snowy    = 0.0  0.1  0.0  0.0
taiga    = 0.0  0.4  0.1  0.0
mountain = 0.0  0.4  0.1  0.0
any      = 0.3  1.0  0.7  0.0
